// Pathways Arabic & Quran Proficiency Exam (RAQP) — Question Bank
// Reference frameworks: CEFR (Council of Europe), ACTFL proficiency guidelines,
// Cambridge English level-differentiated suites, ETS item-writing conventions.
// Source materials: Al-Arabiyya Bayna Yadayk, Al-Kitaab, Madinah Arabic, Duruus al-Lugha.
//
// ── DESIGN RULE (two independent axes) ───────────────────────────────────────
// LEVEL (L1→L4) sets LINGUISTIC DIFFICULTY and is the only difficulty axis.
//   L1 = A1   L2 = A2   L3 = B1   L4 = B2
// SCHOOL TYPE sets TOPIC DOMAIN AND REGISTER ONLY — never difficulty.
//   A1 for an adult and A1 for a 7-year-old are the same linguistic demand;
//   they differ in subject matter (workplace/travel vs. family/animals).
// This mirrors how Cambridge separates YLE from KET/PET at the same CEFR band.
//
// ── ITEM-WRITING RULES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1. Test Arabic. No item may be answerable from English, general knowledge,
//    or trivia (author names, publication facts, dates of revelation).
// 2. Grammatical metalanguage (إعراب terms, صرف patterns, بلاغة labels) is a
//    B1+ construct. At A1/A2 grammar is tested by USE, not by naming the rule.
// 3. Exactly one defensible key. Distractors must be plausible and non-overlapping.
// 4. Options are matched in length. The key must never be the longest option.
// 5. Key positions are distributed across indices 0–3, not clustered.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

